arrowRetiredT -- SEQ 597 R0 (Tony, 2026-11-10): <- retires as an operator. It did nothing := cannot. A <- written as an
operator refuses by name on both roads, "REFUSED <- -- retired as an operator -- write := (SEQ 597)", and stores
nothing. The <- inside a for loop is grammar text, not the operator, and still loops backwards. Rows prefixed AR-.

Not run alone: pop.sh appends the row, print "Q", a sentinel; one process per row. Each value reads: stdout (lines joined
by |) / retired <lines with the retired wording> other <other REFUSED lines> x<exit> s<sentinel>.

AR-1  old road, top level: the target keeps its 2; the statements after it run.
AR-2  new road, through FILe: refused when the statement runs; the rest of that FILe call does not run (an engine
      refusal ends its unit), the statement after the call does.
AR-3  an old-road action, interpreted: refused, and the action ends there (6 is not printed).
AR-4  the same action compiled (testing()).
AR-5  a new-road action body.
AR-6  an argument target: today it refuses as a rebind of the binding (one other REFUSED line); retired, it refuses as
      retired. The caller is unmoved either way.
AR-7  control: the := twin holds 3, in an action and at top level.
AR-8  control: for .. in <- .. loops backwards on both roads.

ROWS, separated by ^ -- id ^ statements ^ value today ^ value intended
